    "knowledge_point": "Integrating Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine",
    "problem": "$\\int 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
